insert into kairo.questions (id, subject, topic, subtopic, learning_objective, concepts_tested, difficulty_rating, cognitive_level, estimated_solving_time_sec, reading_load, calculation_load, distractors, source, year, exam_body, stem, options, correct_option, explanation, lifecycle_state, distractor_rationale) values
($q$chemistry_0481$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Electrochemistry$q$, $q$Industrial electrolysis (chlor-alkali process)$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Industrial electrolysis (chlor-alkali process).$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "23bs2u", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Zinc is reactive enough that it would dissolve into the electrolyte itself as the anode, rather than staying inert and letting chlorine discharge.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Platinum actually is inert enough to work chemically, but it's far too expensive to use on the industrial scale of brine electrolysis - so it's not the practical choice, even though it could function.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Copper would react and dissolve into the solution as Cu2+ ions instead of remaining inert, disrupting the intended electrode reaction.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$In the electrolysis of brine, the anode is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Zinc", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Platinum", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Carbon", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "Copper", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$The trick here is remembering that an anode in this kind of electrolysis must be inert - it shouldn't react with the electrolyte or interfere with the products. Carbon (graphite) fits perfectly: it won't dissolve, and it lets chlorine gas be discharged cleanly at the anode. That's why graphite electrodes were the traditional choice in industrial brine electrolysis.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Zinc is reactive enough that it would dissolve into the electrolyte itself as the anode, rather than staying inert and letting chlorine discharge. B: Platinum actually is inert enough to work chemically, but it's far too expensive to use on the industrial scale of brine electrolysis - so it's not the practical choice, even though it could function. D: Copper would react and dissolve into the solution as Cu2+ ions instead of remaining inert, disrupting the intended electrode reaction.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0482$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Equilibrium$q$, $q$Le Chatelier's principle - pressure effects$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Le Chatelier's principle - pressure effects.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kzcd9t", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Decreasing the volume actually raises the pressure, which pushes the equilibrium the other way - toward the side with fewer moles (the N2O4 reactant), not more product.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Increasing pressure has the same effect as shrinking the volume - it favours the side with fewer gas moles, which works against product formation here.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Keeping conditions constant means there's no change to trigger a shift at all - it doesn't actively favour product formation in either direction.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$N2O4(g) ⇌ 2NO2(g). In the endothermic reaction above, more product formation will be favoured by$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "a decrease in pressure", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "a decrease in volume", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "an increase in pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "a constant volume", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Count the moles of gas on each side before touching Le Chatelier's principle: 1 mole of N2O4 makes 2 moles of NO2, so the product side has more gas particles. Lowering the pressure gives the system 'room to expand', and it responds by shifting toward the side with more moles - the product side. So decreasing pressure favours more NO2.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Decreasing the volume actually raises the pressure, which pushes the equilibrium the other way - toward the side with fewer moles (the N2O4 reactant), not more product. C: Increasing pressure has the same effect as shrinking the volume - it favours the side with fewer gas moles, which works against product formation here. D: Keeping conditions constant means there's no change to trigger a shift at all - it doesn't actively favour product formation in either direction.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0483$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Redox Chemistry$q$, $q$Oxidation states in oxoacids$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Oxidation states in oxoacids.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "y963w7", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This is chlorine's oxidation state in the chloride ion or HCl - it's easy to default to this familiar value instead of working through the oxoacid's actual oxygen count.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "This value comes from miscounting the oxygens (e.g. using only 3 instead of 4) when balancing the equation to zero.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This is chlorine's oxidation state in hypochlorous acid (HOCl), not perchloric acid - the various chlorine oxoacids are easy to mix up.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The oxidation state of chlorine in HClO4 is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "-1", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "+5", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "+7", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "+1", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Set this up like a small equation. H is always +1, and O is almost always -2 - here there are four oxygens, so that's -8 total. The whole compound is neutral, so everything must sum to zero: (+1) + Cl + (-8) = 0, which gives Cl = +7.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This is chlorine's oxidation state in the chloride ion or HCl - it's easy to default to this familiar value instead of working through the oxoacid's actual oxygen count. B: This value comes from miscounting the oxygens (e.g. using only 3 instead of 4) when balancing the equation to zero. D: This is chlorine's oxidation state in hypochlorous acid (HOCl), not perchloric acid - the various chlorine oxoacids are easy to mix up.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0484$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Thermodynamics$q$, $q$Entropy trends across a group$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Entropy trends across a group.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "s55vck", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "HBr is heavier than HF and HCl, but it's still lighter than HI, so its entropy falls short of the top spot.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "HF is the smallest and lightest of these molecules (and its strong hydrogen bonding adds extra order), making it the one with the LOWEST entropy here, not the highest.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "HCl is lighter than both HBr and HI, so it doesn't reach the highest entropy in this group either.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following hydrogen halides has the highest entropy value?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "HBr", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "HF", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "HI", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "HCl", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Entropy is really a measure of disorder and the number of ways energy can be spread out. Heavier, larger molecules have more electrons and more accessible vibrational/rotational states, so their entropy tends to be higher. Among the hydrogen halides, HI has the largest, heaviest halogen atom, giving it the highest standard molar entropy of the group.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: HBr is heavier than HF and HCl, but it's still lighter than HI, so its entropy falls short of the top spot. B: HF is the smallest and lightest of these molecules (and its strong hydrogen bonding adds extra order), making it the one with the LOWEST entropy here, not the highest. D: HCl is lighter than both HBr and HI, so it doesn't reach the highest entropy in this group either.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0485$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Electrochemistry$q$, $q$Faraday's laws of electrolysis$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Faraday's laws of electrolysis.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "cbvosp", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 72, $q$low$q$, $q$heavy$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "This is exactly half the correct answer - a common slip is treating silver as if it needed 2 electrons per ion deposited (like a divalent metal) instead of the correct 1 electron per Ag+ ion.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This is a quarter of the correct value, which can happen if the divalent-ion mistake above is compounded with another halving slip somewhere in the arithmetic.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This is simply silver's molar mass on its own - it comes from forgetting to actually multiply by the 0.5 mol of electrons calculated, and just reporting 108 directly.", "misconceptionId": "rushed_under_pressure"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The mass of silver deposited when a current of 10A is passed through a solution of silver salt for 4830s. [Ag = 108, F = 96500 C mol-1]$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "54.0g", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "27.0g", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "13.5g", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "108.0g", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Work through this in two clean stages rather than jumping straight to mass. First, find the charge passed: Q = I x t = 10A x 4830s = 48,300 C. Next, convert that charge into moles of electrons using the Faraday constant: moles e- = 48,300 / 96,500 = 0.5 mol. Since Ag+ + e- -> Ag is a 1:1 ratio, that's also 0.5 mol of silver. Multiply by silver's molar mass: 0.5 x 108 = 54.0g.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: This is exactly half the correct answer - a common slip is treating silver as if it needed 2 electrons per ion deposited (like a divalent metal) instead of the correct 1 electron per Ag+ ion. C: This is a quarter of the correct value, which can happen if the divalent-ion mistake above is compounded with another halving slip somewhere in the arithmetic. D: This is simply silver's molar mass on its own - it comes from forgetting to actually multiply by the 0.5 mol of electrons calculated, and just reporting 108 directly.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0486$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Redox Chemistry$q$, $q$Dual-role oxidizing/reducing agents$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Dual-role oxidizing/reducing agents.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "0wt48x", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "In H2S, sulphur is already at its lowest common oxidation state (-2), so it has nowhere to go but up - it can really only act as a reducing agent.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "In CO2, carbon is already at its highest common oxidation state (+4), so CO2 struggles to be oxidized further - it's a weak oxidizing agent at best, not a flexible dual-role species.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Hydrogen here is at oxidation state 0, and H2 overwhelmingly acts as a reducing agent, giving up electrons to become H+ rather than swinging both ways.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following acts as both a reducing and an oxidizing agent?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "H2S", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "CO2", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "H2", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "SO2", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Look at where the key atom sits on its oxidation-state 'ladder'. In SO2, sulphur is at +4 - right in the middle of the range sulphur can adopt (from -2 up to +6). That gives it room to move both ways: it can be oxidized further (up to +6, as in H2SO4) or reduced (down toward 0 or lower), which is exactly why it can act as either a reducing or an oxidizing agent depending on what it reacts with.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: In H2S, sulphur is already at its lowest common oxidation state (-2), so it has nowhere to go but up - it can really only act as a reducing agent. B: In CO2, carbon is already at its highest common oxidation state (+4), so CO2 struggles to be oxidized further - it's a weak oxidizing agent at best, not a flexible dual-role species. C: Hydrogen here is at oxidation state 0, and H2 overwhelmingly acts as a reducing agent, giving up electrons to become H+ rather than swinging both ways.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0487$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Equilibrium$q$, $q$Le Chatelier's principle - equal moles of gas$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Le Chatelier's principle - equal moles of gas.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kzcd9t", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$medium$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Here the moles change from 2 to 3, so this equilibrium definitely shifts with pressure (toward the fewer-mole side, O3, when compressed) - the mole-counting check was skipped.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Moles change from 2 to 1 here, so this equilibrium shifts strongly toward N2O4 when compressed - it fails the 'equal moles' test needed for no shift.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Moles change from 1 to 2 here, so compressing the system shifts this equilibrium noticeably back toward PCl5 - again, the mole count on each side isn't equal.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following shows little or no net reaction when the volume of the system is decreased?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "2O3(g) ⇌ 3O2(g)", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "H2(g) + I2(g) ⇌ 2HI(g)", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "2NO2(g) ⇌ N2O4(g)", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "PCl5(g) ⇌ PCl3(g) + Cl2(g)", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Before worrying about direction, just count the moles of gas on each side of each equation. Pressure changes only shift an equilibrium when the two sides have different numbers of gas moles. For H2 + I2 ⇌ 2HI, there are 2 moles of gas on both sides - so squeezing the volume doesn't favour either direction, and you barely see a shift.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Here the moles change from 2 to 3, so this equilibrium definitely shifts with pressure (toward the fewer-mole side, O3, when compressed) - the mole-counting check was skipped. C: Moles change from 2 to 1 here, so this equilibrium shifts strongly toward N2O4 when compressed - it fails the 'equal moles' test needed for no shift. D: Moles change from 1 to 2 here, so compressing the system shifts this equilibrium noticeably back toward PCl5 - again, the mole count on each side isn't equal.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0488$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Thermochemistry$q$, $q$Enthalpy change of reaction calculations$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Enthalpy change of reaction calculations.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "ji3evn", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$application$q$, 72, $q$low$q$, $q$heavy$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This figure comes from an arithmetic misstep while combining the two given values - it doesn't correspond to a clean, correct substitution into ΔH = ΔH(products) - ΔH(reactants).", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This has the right size but the wrong sign - forgetting that forming CO2 (a stable, low-energy compound) from CO releases energy rather than absorbing it.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This combines both a scaling error and a sign error, landing far from the correct value in both size and direction.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$2CO(g) + O2(g) → 2CO2(g). Given that ΔH[CO] is -110.4 kJmol-1 and ΔH[CO2] is -393.0 kJmol-1, the energy change for the reaction above is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "-503.7 kJ", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "-282.6 kJ", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "+282.6 kJ", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "+503.7 kJ", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Apply ΔH = ΔH(products) - ΔH(reactants) using the enthalpy values exactly as given in the question: ΔH = -393.0 - (-110.4 + 0) = -393.0 + 110.4 = -282.6 kJ. (Worth flagging for your own understanding: if you rigorously scale every term by the equation's coefficient of 2, as in a textbook Hess's-law setup, you'd get -566.2 kJ instead - but that figure isn't offered as a choice here, so work with the numbers exactly as the question presents them.)$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This figure comes from an arithmetic misstep while combining the two given values - it doesn't correspond to a clean, correct substitution into ΔH = ΔH(products) - ΔH(reactants). C: This has the right size but the wrong sign - forgetting that forming CO2 (a stable, low-energy compound) from CO releases energy rather than absorbing it. D: This combines both a scaling error and a sign error, landing far from the correct value in both size and direction.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0489$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Redox Chemistry$q$, $q$Identifying oxidation and reduction in a reaction$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Identifying oxidation and reduction in a reaction.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "3hq7md", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "'Displaced' describes what happens when one element pushes another out of a compound in a displacement reaction - it's not the specific redox description (oxidation vs reduction) the question is asking for.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "This reverses the actual direction of change - zinc's oxidation number decreases here (from +2 to 0), which is reduction, not oxidation.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Decomposition means one substance breaking apart into multiple simpler products - but zinc isn't the substance splitting apart here, it's simply changing oxidation state.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$ZnO + CO → Zn + CO2. In the reaction above, zinc has been$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "displaced", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "oxidized", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "reduced", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "decomposed", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Track zinc's oxidation number through the reaction rather than just looking at the equation shape. In ZnO, zinc is +2. In elemental Zn, it's 0. That drop in oxidation number means zinc gained electrons - and gaining electrons is the definition of reduction. (CO, meanwhile, is oxidized to CO2, acting as the reducing agent that supplies those electrons.)$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: 'Displaced' describes what happens when one element pushes another out of a compound in a displacement reaction - it's not the specific redox description (oxidation vs reduction) the question is asking for. B: This reverses the actual direction of change - zinc's oxidation number decreases here (from +2 to 0), which is reduction, not oxidation. D: Decomposition means one substance breaking apart into multiple simpler products - but zinc isn't the substance splitting apart here, it's simply changing oxidation state.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0490$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Quantitative Chemistry$q$, $q$Stoichiometry - gas volume calculations at s.t.p.$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Stoichiometry - gas volume calculations at s.t.p..$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "v4o1jb", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 72, $q$low$q$, $q$heavy$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This is exactly half the correct value - it can happen if the CaCO3:CO2 mole ratio is mistakenly treated as 2:1 instead of the correct 1:1.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "This is a quarter of the correct value, consistent with stacking two separate halving mistakes in the working.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This is ten times too large - a classic sign of a decimal-place or unit slip when converting between dm3 and cm3 for the molar volume.", "misconceptionId": "unit_conversion_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$What volume of gas is evolved at s.t.p. if 2g of calcium trioxocarbonate(IV) is added to a solution of hydrochloric acid? [Ca = 40, C = 12, O = 16, Cl = 35.5, H = 1, molar volume of a gas at s.t.p = 22.4 dm3]$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "224cm3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "112cm3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "2240cm3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "448cm3", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Start from the balanced equation: CaCO3 + 2HCl → CaCl2 + H2O + CO2. Molar mass of CaCO3 = 40 + 12 + (16x3) = 100 g/mol, so 2g is 0.02 mol. The CaCO3-to-CO2 ratio is 1:1, so you also get 0.02 mol of CO2. At s.t.p., 1 mole of any gas occupies 22.4 dm3 = 22,400 cm3, so the volume of CO2 = 0.02 x 22,400 = 448 cm3.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This is exactly half the correct value - it can happen if the CaCO3:CO2 mole ratio is mistakenly treated as 2:1 instead of the correct 1:1. B: This is a quarter of the correct value, consistent with stacking two separate halving mistakes in the working. C: This is ten times too large - a classic sign of a decimal-place or unit slip when converting between dm3 and cm3 for the molar volume.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0491$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Nature of Matter$q$, $q$Defining features of a chemical reaction$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Defining features of a chemical reaction.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "vvwi6v", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This sounds close to right, but a physical change - like ice melting - also changes a substance's 'nature' in some loose sense without being a chemical reaction at all, so it's not the precise defining feature.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Plenty of reactions, especially in solution or between solids, show little or no noticeable change in volume - so this isn't something you can always count on.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This phrase doesn't correspond to a real, general description of what happens in a chemical reaction - it's easy to pick when unsure, without it actually explaining anything.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$A chemical reaction is always associated with$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "a change in the nature of the reactants", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "the formation of new substances", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "a change in the volume of the reactants", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "an increase in the composition of one of the substances", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Strip this back to the core definition: what separates a chemical reaction from a physical change is that the atoms rearrange into genuinely new substances with different properties - not just a different shape, state, or volume of the same substance. That's the one thing that's always true, every single time.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This sounds close to right, but a physical change - like ice melting - also changes a substance's 'nature' in some loose sense without being a chemical reaction at all, so it's not the precise defining feature. C: Plenty of reactions, especially in solution or between solids, show little or no noticeable change in volume - so this isn't something you can always count on. D: This phrase doesn't correspond to a real, general description of what happens in a chemical reaction - it's easy to pick when unsure, without it actually explaining anything.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0492$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Separation Techniques$q$, $q$Sublimation$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Sublimation.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kc1jua", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Crystallization runs in the opposite direction - a dissolved substance or vapour forming a solid - not a solid turning into gas.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Distillation is a technique for separating liquids based on boiling points, involving a liquid boiling and its vapour condensing elsewhere - it doesn't describe a solid vanishing into gas.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Evaporation describes a liquid turning to gas at its surface, not a solid doing so - the starting state is the key difference here.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$When a solid substance disappears completely as a gas on heating, the substance is said to have undergone$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "sublimation", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "crystallization", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "distillation", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "evaporation", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$The giveaway phrase is 'disappears completely as a gas' - going straight from solid to gas, skipping the liquid stage entirely. That direct solid-to-gas jump is the definition of sublimation, seen with substances like iodine or ammonium chloride.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Crystallization runs in the opposite direction - a dissolved substance or vapour forming a solid - not a solid turning into gas. C: Distillation is a technique for separating liquids based on boiling points, involving a liquid boiling and its vapour condensing elsewhere - it doesn't describe a solid vanishing into gas. D: Evaporation describes a liquid turning to gas at its surface, not a solid doing so - the starting state is the key difference here.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0493$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Quantitative Chemistry$q$, $q$Stoichiometry - acid-base reacting masses$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Stoichiometry - acid-base reacting masses.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "e5q9c8", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 72, $q$low$q$, $q$heavy$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This is ten times too large - a sign of a decimal-place slip somewhere in the moles-to-grams conversion.", "misconceptionId": "unit_conversion_error"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "This is simply CuO's full molar mass on its own - it comes from forgetting to multiply by the correctly-found 0.05 mol and just reporting the molar mass directly.", "misconceptionId": "rushed_under_pressure"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This is one-tenth of the correct value, the opposite decimal-place slip from option A.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$If a solution contains 4.9g of tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid, calculate the amount of copper(II) oxide that will react with it. [Cu = 64, O = 16, S = 32, H = 1]$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "40.0g", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "80.0g", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "0.8g", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "4.0g", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Start with the balanced equation: CuO + H2SO4 → CuSO4 + H2O, a clean 1:1 mole ratio. Molar mass of H2SO4 = 2 + 32 + 64 = 98 g/mol, so 4.9g is 4.9 / 98 = 0.05 mol. Since the ratio is 1:1, you also need 0.05 mol of CuO. Molar mass of CuO = 64 + 16 = 80 g/mol, so mass = 0.05 x 80 = 4.0g.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This is ten times too large - a sign of a decimal-place slip somewhere in the moles-to-grams conversion. B: This is simply CuO's full molar mass on its own - it comes from forgetting to multiply by the correctly-found 0.05 mol and just reporting the molar mass directly. C: This is one-tenth of the correct value, the opposite decimal-place slip from option A.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0494$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Rubber chemistry and vulcanization$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Rubber chemistry and vulcanization.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "b3vqsm", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This reverses which bond type is actually involved - it's the double bonds that get consumed during cross-linking, not single bonds.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "The rubber polymer itself is what's being modified by vulcanization, not something being removed from it.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Vulcanization acts on an already-formed polymer chain through cross-linking; it isn't about removing individual monomer units.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Vulcanization involves the removal of$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "the single bond", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "a double bond", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "a polymer", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "a monomer", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Picture what's happening at the molecular level: natural rubber (polyisoprene) is full of C=C double bonds along its chain. During vulcanization, sulphur atoms form cross-links right at those double bond sites, converting them into single bonds bridged by sulphur. That cross-linking is what makes vulcanized rubber stronger and more elastic - and it works by consuming the double bonds.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This reverses which bond type is actually involved - it's the double bonds that get consumed during cross-linking, not single bonds. C: The rubber polymer itself is what's being modified by vulcanization, not something being removed from it. D: Vulcanization acts on an already-formed polymer chain through cross-linking; it isn't about removing individual monomer units.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0495$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$General formulae of hydrocarbon series$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on General formulae of hydrocarbon series.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "bo2kcr", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "CnH2n is the general formula for alkenes (one C=C double bond), a different series with a different degree of unsaturation.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "CnH2n-2 is the general formula for alkynes (one C≡C triple bond) - two hydrogens fewer than the alkane, not one.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "CnH2n+2 is the formula for the parent alkane itself, before a hydrogen is removed to create the alkyl group.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The alkyl group can be represented by the general formula$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "CnH2n", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "CnH2n-2", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "CnH2n+1", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "CnH2n+2", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Build this from something you already know: alkanes follow CnH2n+2. An alkyl group is just an alkane with one hydrogen removed (that's the open bond it uses to attach elsewhere), so it has exactly one fewer hydrogen: CnH2n+1.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: CnH2n is the general formula for alkenes (one C=C double bond), a different series with a different degree of unsaturation. B: CnH2n-2 is the general formula for alkynes (one C≡C triple bond) - two hydrogens fewer than the alkane, not one. D: CnH2n+2 is the formula for the parent alkane itself, before a hydrogen is removed to create the alkyl group.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0496$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Dehydration of alcohols (temperature-dependent products)$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Dehydration of alcohols (temperature-dependent products).$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "so95ev", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Propanoic acid isn't a plausible product of dehydrating ethanol at all - nothing in this reaction adds a carbon or an extra oxidation step.", "misconceptionId": "guessed_no_reasoning"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Methane has fewer carbons than ethanol and would require breaking the C-C bond, which this dehydration reaction doesn't do.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Ether formation is the outcome favoured at the LOWER temperature (around 140°C) with excess ethanol - at 180°C, the reaction shifts toward the alkene instead.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$C2H5OH(aq) --[Conc. H2SO4 / 180°C]--> Y. In the reaction above, Y represents$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "C2H5COOH", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "CH4", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "CH3OCH3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "C2H4", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$This is where the temperature detail matters more than students often expect: concentrated H2SO4 with ethanol behaves differently depending on how hot you go. At the higher temperature (around 180°C), the reaction favours elimination (dehydration) of a water molecule, producing the alkene ethene, C2H4. (Lower down, around 140°C with excess alcohol, the same reagents instead favour forming an ether.)$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Propanoic acid isn't a plausible product of dehydrating ethanol at all - nothing in this reaction adds a carbon or an extra oxidation step. B: Methane has fewer carbons than ethanol and would require breaking the C-C bond, which this dehydration reaction doesn't do. C: Ether formation is the outcome favoured at the LOWER temperature (around 140°C) with excess ethanol - at 180°C, the reaction shifts toward the alkene instead.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0497$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Everyday Chemistry$q$, $q$Soap manufacture (salting out)$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Soap manufacture (salting out).$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "gqm7dj", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Saponification is the earlier reaction step (fat/oil + NaOH → soap + glycerol) - the NaCl is added afterward, for a different purpose.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Emulsifying grease and oil is what soap DOES when you use it to wash - it's not what's happening to the soap itself during manufacture.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This is the reverse of what actually happens - NaCl decreases the soap's solubility, causing it to separate out, not dissolve further.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$In the production of soap, concentrated sodium chloride is added to$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "saponify the soap", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "emulsify the soap", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "decrease the solubility of the soap", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "increase the solubility of the soap", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$This step is called 'salting out'. Once the soap has already formed, adding concentrated NaCl sharply reduces how much of it can stay dissolved, so it precipitates out as a solid curd that floats free of the glycerol-and-water mixture, ready to be skimmed off and separated.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Saponification is the earlier reaction step (fat/oil + NaOH → soap + glycerol) - the NaCl is added afterward, for a different purpose. B: Emulsifying grease and oil is what soap DOES when you use it to wash - it's not what's happening to the soap itself during manufacture. D: This is the reverse of what actually happens - NaCl decreases the soap's solubility, causing it to separate out, not dissolve further.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0498$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Industrial Chemistry$q$, $q$Combustion reactions and welding$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Combustion reactions and welding.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "jv7ln2", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "This isn't the actual combustion chemistry at play - burning acetylene in oxygen produces CO2 and water, and this option's process doesn't even generate the heat needed for welding.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Welding requires melting the metal at the joint, not solidifying it quickly - that would work against the whole point of welding.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "A 'pop' sound on ignition is the classic test associated with hydrogen gas, not the reason oxyacetylene is chosen for welding.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Oxyacetylene flame is used for iron-welding because it$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "evolves a lot of heat when burnt", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "dissociates to produce carbon(IV) oxide and oxygen", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "makes the iron metal solidify very quickly", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "combines with oxygen to give a pop sound", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Welding needs enough energy to actually melt metal, and that's exactly what oxyacetylene delivers - burning acetylene in pure oxygen is intensely exothermic, reaching flame temperatures around 3000°C, hot enough to melt and fuse iron at the joint.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: This isn't the actual combustion chemistry at play - burning acetylene in oxygen produces CO2 and water, and this option's process doesn't even generate the heat needed for welding. C: Welding requires melting the metal at the joint, not solidifying it quickly - that would work against the whole point of welding. D: A 'pop' sound on ignition is the classic test associated with hydrogen gas, not the reason oxyacetylene is chosen for welding.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0499$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Tests for unsaturation - confirming a triple bond$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Tests for unsaturation - confirming a triple bond.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "vfpcni", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Bromine gas decolorizes in the presence of both alkenes and alkynes, so it's a general unsaturation test, not one that specifically pins down a triple bond.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Bromine water has the same limitation as bromine gas - it reacts with C=C double bonds just as readily, so it can't distinguish a double bond from a triple bond.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Acidified KMnO4 also decolorizes in the presence of C=C double bonds, making it another general test for unsaturation rather than something specific to triple bonds.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of these reagents can confirm the presence of a triple bond?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Bromine gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Bromine water", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Acidified KMnO4", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Copper(I) chloride", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$The word 'confirm' is doing the heavy lifting here - you need a test that's specific to a triple bond, not one that also lights up for a double bond. Bromine (gas or water) and acidified KMnO4 both react with any C=C double bond too, so a positive result there doesn't tell you which kind of unsaturation you have. Ammoniacal copper(I) chloride is different: it reacts specifically with terminal alkynes, forming a distinctive red precipitate of copper acetylide - a positive result here really does confirm a triple bond.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Bromine gas decolorizes in the presence of both alkenes and alkynes, so it's a general unsaturation test, not one that specifically pins down a triple bond. B: Bromine water has the same limitation as bromine gas - it reacts with C=C double bonds just as readily, so it can't distinguish a double bond from a triple bond. C: Acidified KMnO4 also decolorizes in the presence of C=C double bonds, making it another general test for unsaturation rather than something specific to triple bonds.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0500$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$IUPAC nomenclature of branched alkanes$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on IUPAC nomenclature of branched alkanes.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "r4t0dz", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$medium$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This comes from misreading exactly which carbons along the drawn chain carry the branch substituents, shifting the numbering by a position.", "misconceptionId": "misread_graph"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This treats the two separate CH3 branches as if they were a single larger ethyl group, rather than correctly reading them as two independent methyl substituents at neighbouring carbons.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This miscounts the main chain as only five carbons (pentane) instead of six (hexane), on top of misreading the branch type - two structural misreadings stacked together.", "misconceptionId": "misread_graph"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Given the structure:
